<!doctype html>
<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Resume over the ring</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css"></head>
<body><article class="card ">
<header><span class="eyebrow"><b>Complete the print</b> &middot; build guide</span><h1>Resume over the ring</h1><i class="rule"></i><p class="lede">The same ASA Aero job builds the material above the pocket and closes the ring inside the body.</p></header>
<main><figure class="mid"><img src="art/roof.png" alt="Section through the current body. Coral shows the material printed above the pocket; the ring is centered at the body&#x27;s axial midplane."><figcaption>Section through the current body. Coral shows the material printed above the pocket; the ring is centered at the body's axial midplane.</figcaption></figure><ol class="actions"><li>Remove hands, tools and loose strings from the printer. Close the enclosure.</li><li>Manually resume the same job. Let the printer restore its temperatures and continue the covering roads.</li><li>Observe the initial covering layers for ring movement, dragging or lifting. Record the result with that article.</li></ol><div class="mind"><span class="mind-label">What the existing print demonstrates</span><p>The identified v1 article supports paused insertion and the initial covering deposition. It does not establish the finished roof or retained signal for a cooled float.</p></div><p class="dim">RC62 has an 80 &deg;C continuous operating limit. The 270 &deg;C nozzle setting does not tell us the magnet's actual temperature; retained field after insertion is unmeasured.</p></main><footer><span>ASA Aero magnetic float &middot; Home Soda Machine</span><span class="folio">8</span></footer>
</article></body></html>